s strong consistency but can reduce concurrency and risk deadlocks. Optimistic locking assumes conflicts are rare and does not lock during reads; instead, it validates at write time by checking a version number or timestamp. If the version has changed since the read, the update is rejected (often with a retry). This approach maximizes concurrency and avoids lock overhead, but requires handling conflicts and may be unsuitable for high-contention scenarios. Key differences: locking timing (before vs. after), concurrency impact, conflict handling, and suitability for read-heavy vs. write-heavy workloads. Choose pessimistic when data consistency is critical and contention is high; choose optimistic when read/write ratios are high and retries are acceptable.","expected_type":null,"description":null},"parse_error":null}],"events":{"before_count":12,"after_count":18,"new":[{"at_utc":"2026-08-20T07:12:25.928840+00:00","kind":"OPERATION_MATERIALIZED","payload":{"operation":"INTERPRET_PLAN_REVIEW","stage":"40_plan_review","invocation_id":"0004-interpret_plan_review"}},{"at_utc":"2026-08-20T07:12:26.061182+00:00","kind":"MODEL_OUTPUT_RECORDED","payload":{"operation":"INTERPRET_PLAN_REVIEW","stage":"40_plan_review","invocation_id":"0004-interpret_plan_review"}},{"at_utc":"2026-08-20T07:12:26.091647+00:00","kind":"ARTIFACT_CONFIRMED","payload":{"kind":"plan","artifact_id":"I-9507ab0046-R1"}},{"at_utc":"2026-08-20T07:12:26.297328+00:00","kind":"OPERATION_MATERIALIZED","payload":{"operation":"EXECUTE","stage":"50_execution","invocation_id":"0005-execute"}},{"at_utc":"2026-08-20T07:12:26.332773+00:00","kind":"MODEL_OUTPUT_RECORDED","payload":{"operation":"EXECUTE","stage":"50_execution","invocation_id":"0005-execute"}},{"at_utc":"2026-08-20T07:12:26.436655+00:00","kind":"EXECUTION_OUTCOME_PUBLISHED","payload":{"kind":"RESULT"}}]},"exception":null,"observer_analysis_error":null,"wall_ms":594.1667000006419}
